Apache Ant @VERSION@ for OpenVMS (@KITVERSION@, @ARCH@)
===================================================

Apache Ant (https://ant.apache.org) running on VSI OpenJDK 8.  This kit is
the upstream binary distribution with six patched classes in lib/ant.jar,
ANT.EXE, a launcher that does what bin/ant does, and ANTDCL.EXE, which runs
the DCL commands a build starts.  Sources, patches and notes:
https://github.com/issinoho/vms-ant

Installation
------------

    $ PRODUCT INSTALL ANT /SOURCE=<directory with the kit>

The Ant home is ANT$ROOT:[000000] (the installed [ANT] directory).  To define
ANT$ROOT at every boot, add to SYS$MANAGER:SYSTARTUP_VMS.COM:

    $ @SYS$STARTUP:ANT$STARTUP.COM

and for each user, to LOGIN.COM (or SYS$MANAGER:SYLOGIN.COM):

    $ @ANT$ROOT:[000000]ANT$SETUP.COM

ANT$SETUP.COM defines ANT and, when no Java is set up, runs
SYS$STARTUP:OPENJDK$SETUP.COM.  PRODUCT REMOVE ANT removes it all.  The manual
is ANT$ROOT:[MANUAL]index.html.

Using it
--------

    $ SET PROCESS/PARSE_STYLE=EXTENDED       ! keep the case of arguments
    $ ANT                                    ! build.xml in the current directory
    $ PIPE ANT -f /DISK$USER/ME/proj/build.xml dist > build.log

- Under the TRADITIONAL parse style (the default, and what batch jobs get),
  arguments not in quotes reach Ant in lower case: "-Dmy.Prop=Value" and
  target names with capitals need the quotes.  The launcher prints a note
  when Ant then fails.
- Give file options Unix paths (-f /DISK$USER/ME/proj/build.xml); Java on
  OpenVMS does not take [.X]Y.Z.  Paths in build files are Unix paths too.
- Programs a build starts:
    * <java fork>, <javac fork>, <junit fork>, <javadoc> run the JDK's images
      (JAVA.EXE, JAVAC.EXE...) with their arguments as given.
    * <exec executable="mms"> runs a DCL command when no image of that name
      exists: DCL verbs and the job's foreign-command symbols work.
      vmlauncher="false" always uses DCL; vmlauncher="true" never does.
      An argument with white space is quoted for DCL; one written with
      quotes already ('"Two words"') and qualifiers (/SIZE) are passed as they
      are.  A DCL command gets its input from NL:.
    * A program's exit code decides success, as on other systems: 0 is
      success.  A DCL command's success status counts as 0.
- <property environment="env"/> gives the process's logical names
  (env.SYS$LOGIN ...).
- File versions: the launcher has Java delete and rename all versions of a
  file (JAVA$DELETE_ALL_VERSIONS and two more, unless defined), so <delete>
  and clean targets work.  A file a build rewrites in place (reports, <echo
  file>) still gets a new version: SET DIRECTORY/VERSION_LIMIT on output
  directories if that matters.
- Quotas: Java wants FILLM 4096 and PGFLQUOTA 2097152.  Forked JVMs share them
  with Ant's, so with a default account's quotas larger builds fail ("unable
  to create new native thread"); the launcher says so.  ANT_OPTS sets the
  JVM's sizes (the launcher's defaults are -Xms16m -Xmx256m).
- On a terminal, CTRL/C and CTRL/Y stop Ant (Java runs its shutdown hooks),
  also while a DCL command runs.  $STATUS afterwards is not reliable.
- ANT_OPTS, ANT_ARGS, CLASSPATH, JAVA_HOME and ANT_HOME may be DCL symbols or
  logical names; --noclasspath leaves CLASSPATH out.  ANT_VMS_TRACE prints
  the Java command and each DCL command ANTDCL.EXE runs.  -lib and
  SYS$LOGIN:[.ant.lib] add jars to Ant, as elsewhere.

Not in the kit: the Unix and Windows scripts of bin/ (ant, ant.bat, antRun,
runant.pl ...).

